3. COPYLEFT AND LICENSE CONDITIONS

Sections 3 through 9 are conditions of the grant of this License. Any act that does not meet these conditions falls outside the scope of the authorization of this License.

3.1 Retention of Notices. Any copies, modified versions, Derivative Works, or substantial portions must retain this copyright notice, the full text of this License, the disclaimer, and references to `DEPLOYMENT-COVENANT.md`, `TRADEMARK-POLICY.md`, and `ENFORCEMENT.md`. Those who modify the Project Materials must clearly indicate the content of the modifications, the date of modification, and the name of the modifier.

3.2 Copyleft Distribution. Any entity distributing the Project Materials or their Derivative Works must license the entire Derivative Work as a whole, free of charge to all recipients under the terms of this License (and only under this License), and simultaneously provide the complete Corresponding Source. Independent proprietary rights cannot be claimed over any part of the Derivative Works to circumvent the Copyleft requirements of this License.

3.3 Network Service Copyleft (SaaS Circumvention Prevention). Any entity providing the functions, interfaces, or inference services of the Project Materials or their Derivative Works to third parties or an unspecified majority internally via a network (including but not limited to the Internet, LAN, Intranet) is deemed to be distributing. The provider must prominently offer to all users interacting with it over the network a means to freely download the complete Corresponding Source of the running version (e.g., providing a standard Git repository link or a one-click download button).

3.4 Isolation Architecture Circumvention Prevention. Claiming that the calling or integrating software is not a Derivative Work through "isolation" architectural designs—such as encapsulating the Project Materials as an independent microservice, placing them in a container or virtual machine, placing them behind a reverse proxy, or communicating with proprietary closed-source software solely via APIs (e.g., REST, gRPC, GraphQL)—is prohibited. As long as the operation of the proprietary software substantially relies on the functions of the Project Materials, the proprietary software, together with the Project Materials, constitutes a Derivative Work subject to this License as a whole, and must be open-sourced in its entirety and comply with this License.

3.5 Prohibition of Additional Restrictions. No additional legal, contractual, or technical restrictions that conflict with this License may be imposed on the Project Materials or their Derivative Works (for example, requiring the signing of a Non-Disclosure Agreement (NDA) to obtain the source code, using Digital Rights Management (DRM) technology to restrict the execution of modified code, or utilizing cloud service terms to override this License).

4. CORE LABOR PROTECTION RESTRICTIONS

4.1 No one shall use the Project Materials for Production Deployment that causes Adverse Labor Measures.

4.2 No one shall use the Project Materials for projects whose goals, metrics, or business plan components include reducing jobs, suppressing wages, lowering the proportion of stable employment, or weakening trade unions or workers' collective bargaining power.

4.3 No one shall use the Project Materials to automate or substantially dominate the following decisions:
(a) Layoffs, dismissals, elimination of posts, or non-renewal of contracts;
(b) Hiring freezes or non-replacement of vacancies;
(c) Pay cuts, reduction of hours, or reduction of benefits;
(d) Performance-based eliminations, disciplinary actions, or scheduling punishments;
(e) Downgrading of position levels, conversion to outsourcing, platformization, or similar destabilizing decisions.

4.4 As long as the Project Materials constitute a substantial factor in a specific labor replacement, job reduction, or deterioration of working conditions, it is deemed a violation of this License; this is not limited to whether it is the sole cause, final decision-making tool, or sole technical component.

4.5 When any deployment entity is in a General Labor Law Disqualified Status, it shall not copy, modify, distribute, host, Network Provide, or Production Deploy the Project Materials; its related rights obtained based on this License are automatically suspended.

4.6 When a China Employing Entity is in a Disqualified Status, it shall not copy, modify, distribute, host, Network Provide, or Production Deploy the Project Materials; its related rights obtained based on this License are automatically suspended.

5. ANTI-CIRCUMVENTION CLAUSES

5.1 Section 4 shall not be circumvented under the guise of "non-replacement of natural attrition", "organizational optimization", "business restructuring", "efficiency improvement", "AI empowerment", "position upgrading", "headcount adjustment", "outsourcing of non-core business", "cost synergy", "supplier replacement", or other names.

5.2 Section 4 shall not be circumvented by splitting, renaming, relocating, converting to contracting, or transferring original positions to affiliated companies or suppliers.

5.3 Adverse Labor Measures that should have been borne by the user itself shall not be implemented through Affiliated Circumvention.

5.4 Within twenty-four months after the deployment of the Project Materials, if there is a decline in Baseline Working Conditions in the relevant function or business unit, it is presumed that the decline is related to the Project Materials; the user bears the burden of rebutting this presumption with complete, contemporaneously formed written evidence.

5.5 If a user refuses to provide records, explanations, or audit cooperation required by this License or `DEPLOYMENT-COVENANT.md`, it is presumed to be in violation of this License.

5.6 China Employing Entities shall not circumvent the Two-Day Weekend Baseline using expressions such as "flexible working", "striver agreements", "voluntary overtime", "rotational culture", "wartime mechanisms", "project sprints", "normalized duty", or similar phrasing.

5.7 China Employing Entities shall not circumvent Section 1.11 by splitting positions into formal and outsourced, onshore and offshore, main contracts and outsourcing agreements, day shifts and night shifts, or R&D and operational support.

6. PRECONDITIONS FOR PRODUCTION DEPLOYMENT

6.1 Prior to any Production Deployment, the user must accept and comply with `DEPLOYMENT-COVENANT.md`.

6.2 Prior to any Production Deployment, the user must form a written Labor Impact Assessment, specifying at least:
(a) The specific functions planned for automation;
(b) The affected positions and headcount;
(c) The expected changes compared to Baseline Working Conditions;
(d) Protection plans such as training, transfer, shortening working hours without pay cuts, or hiring additional auditing positions;
(e) Grievance contact person and contact information;
(f) Deployment version, date, responsible person, and business unit.

6.3 If there is a trade union, employee representatives, or other worker representative organizations, the aforementioned assessment must be provided at least forty-five days prior to deployment and negotiated in good faith.

6.4 Failure to complete the procedures listed in Sections 6.1 through 6.3 prohibits Production Deployment.

6.5 For all deployment entities, in addition to Sections 6.1 through 6.4, they must also form and archive prior to deployment a self-certification of labor law compliance in their applicable jurisdiction, as well as administrative dispositions, judgments, arbitrations, public announcements, and rectification proofs related to major labor law violations over the past twenty-four months (if any).

6.6 For China Employing Entities, in addition to Sections 6.1 through 6.5, they must also form and archive prior to deployment:
(a) A self-certification of China labor law compliance;
(b) An explanation of the execution of the Two-Day Weekend and a list of covered positions;
(c) Statistics on working hours and overtime, arrangements for rest days, and compensatory time off records for the past twelve months;
(d) Approval documents for special working hours (if applicable);
(e) Labor security administrative dispositions, arbitrations, judgments, public announcements, and rectification proofs over the past twenty-four months (if any).

7. PERMITTED AUTOMATION TRANSFORMATIONS

A relevant deployment is not considered a violation of Section 4 ONLY when all the following conditions are met:
(a) No Adverse Labor Measures occur;
(b) Affected workers receive transfer, training, or job restructuring arrangements with no less than their original compensation, benefits, stability, and seniority;
(c) Productivity gains are prioritized for shortening working hours without pay cuts, increasing remuneration, improving safety and working conditions, or increasing manual review or public service capabilities, rather than reducing headcount;
(d) The user retains complete records and can prove the above matters;
(e) If worker representatives exist, they have not explicitly objected, or alternative arrangements have been made through a written collective agreement;
(f) Continuous compliance with applicable labor law requirements;
(g) For China Employing Entities, continuous compliance with the China Labor Law Compliance Status and the Two-Day Weekend Baseline.

8. RECORDS, AUDIT, AND BURDEN OF PROOF

8.1 Users must retain at least the following records for four years:
(a) Deployment version and time;
(b) Affected business units;
(c) Labor Impact Assessment and its updated drafts;
(d) Records of hiring, renewal, elimination, transfer, outsourcing, and compensation adjustments;
(e) Communication records with trade unions or worker representatives;
(f) Logs and institutional documents related to the use of Project Material outputs for personnel decisions;
(g) Attendance records, overtime applications and approvals, and ledgers for overtime pay and compensatory time off;
(h) Two-day weekend scheduling records, on-call standby arrangements, rest day communication notices, and response logs;
(i) Approval documents for special working hours, labor security supervision processing documents, status on public announcement lists, and rectification materials;
(j) Inspection, penalty, public announcement, untrustworthiness, or similar disciplinary status and rectification materials from labor competent authorities in applicable jurisdictions.

8.2 Upon presenting specific clues of a breach, the Copyright Holder or representatives of affected workers have the right to request access to records directly related to the judgment of the breach, subject to necessary desensitization.

8.3 Failure of a user to provide relevant materials within fifteen working days after receiving a reasonable request constitutes an independent breach and triggers the presumption under Section 5.5.

9. TERMINATION AND RESTORATION

9.1 For anyone who violates this License, all rights acquired based on this License terminate automatically.

9.2 In the event of a breach involving workers' interests, unless the breaching party:
(a) Stops the relevant deployment;
(b) Restores the affected workers' positions or provides equivalent positions;
(c) Makes up for wages, benefits, and related losses, or reaches a written remedial agreement with worker representatives;
(d) Completes supplementary disclosure of source code and records;
its rights shall not be restored.

9.3 Upon termination of rights, the breaching party must immediately cease copying, distributing, modifying, hosting, Network Provision, and Production Deployment.

9.4 Any deployment entity during a General Labor Law Disqualified Status shall not claim any rights based on this License; it may only apply to the Copyright Holder for restoration after completing rectification and submitting verifiable proof of compliance.

9.5 For China Employing Entities, during a Disqualified Status, no rights based on this License may be claimed; they may only apply to the Copyright Holder for restoration after completing rectification and continuously meeting the China Labor Law Compliance Status and the Two-Day Weekend Baseline for twelve consecutive months.

